    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How do we know without counting that the number of circles equals the number of squares? Because we can match every circle to exactly one square, and each square has a match:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We say that we have shown that there is a one-to-one correspondence of the set of circles and the set of squares. We can do this with infinite sets, too! For example, there are the same “number” of positive integers as there are even…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Every positive integer is matched to exactly one even positive integer, and every even integer has a match! We have shown that there is a one-to-one correspondence between the set of positive integers and the set of even positive integers.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Show that the set of square roots of positive integers is countable.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Show that the set of positive integer roots of 2 is countable.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Show that the set of positive integer roots of positive integers is countable. (Hint: there is a famous proof that the positive rational numbers are countable. Find and study this proof.)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How can we make sense of the expression \\(11^{\\frac12}\\)? For this expression to make any sense at all, we should be able to apply exponent rules to it. Let’s try squaring \\(11^{\\frac12}\\) using exponent rules: \\(\\left ( 11^{\\frac12}\\right…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Similarly, \\(11^{\\frac13}\\) must be equal to \\(\\sqrt[3]{11}\\) because \\(\\begin{array}{} \\left(11^{\\frac13}\\right)^3 &= 11^{\\frac13 \\boldcdot 3} \\\\ &=11 \\end{array}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In general, if \\(a\\) is any positive number, then",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle a^{\\frac12} = \\sqrt{a}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle a^{\\frac13} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L03-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Exponents and Radicals",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Remember, these expressions that involve the \\(\\sqrt{}\\) symbol are often referred to as radical expressions.",
